<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assizes of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Assizes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> are a collection of medieval legal treatises setting out the laws and customs of the crusader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. The most important were written in the 13th century by jurists such as Philip of Novara and John of Ibelin, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a>, describing the procedure of the High Court and the rights of the king's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. They are a major source for feudal law in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> East.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assizes-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Assizes of Jerusalem" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_Royaume_de_J%C3%A9rusalem.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The treatises were written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> and survive only from the 13th century, at least a generation after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. The kingdom's earliest laws were issued at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> in 1120 but seem to have fallen out of use. No laws or court cases survive from the 12th century, though the kingdom clearly had a well-developed legal structure.</p><p>Philip of Novara and John of Ibelin recounted the legends that had grown up about it. In their account <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> founded both the Haute Cour and the burgess court in 1099, and their laws were written down from the start and kept in a chest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>, the Letres dou Sepulcre, which only the king, the Patriarch and the viscount of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> could open, until they were lost when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> took the city in 1187. Philip said he had his information from an old knight, Ralph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a>. Peter Edbury, among others, does not believe these legends.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The earliest text, the Livre au Roi of about 1200, was written under Queen Isabella I and King Aimery and has a royalist slant; it alone preserves King Baldwin II's établissement allowing the king to disinherit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> without the judgement of the High Court. Philip of Novara's Livre de Forme de Plait of the 1250s takes a more aristocratic view, and John of Ibelin's Livre des assises, written from 1264 to 1266, is the longest legal treatise of the Latin East or of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>The anonymous Livre des Assises de la Cour des Bourgeois covers the burgess court; according to Joshua Prawer it derives from Lo Codi, a Provençal code based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>. Several texts preserve King Amalric's Assise sur la ligece of the 1170s, which made every lord a direct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Auguste Arthur, comte de Beugnot, edited the texts in the 19th century for the Recueil des Historiens des Croisades, though later editors judge him a poor editor. Myriam Greilshammer published a critical edition of the Livre au Roi in 1995 and Edbury one of John of Ibelin in 2003, while Nicholas Coureas translated the burgess assizes from their 15th-century Greek version. Historians once saw the assizes as the purest form of European feudalism; later scholarship holds that they were written from scratch in the 13th century to look back to the less troubled days of the 12th and present an idealised legal model.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Assizes of Jerusalem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jurists such as John of Ibelin, count of Jaffa, wrote legal treatises later known as the <b>Assizes of Jerusalem</b>, which set out the customs of the High Court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
